THOUGHT: My previous change made `toEncode` nullable, but NullAway now complains about passing a nullable `toEncode` to `DataCacheWriter` and dereferencing it in `encode()`. The intended behavior is that `encode()` is only called when there is a resource to encode, so `toEncode` should be non-null whenever `encoder` is non-null. A safer fix is to keep `toEncode` non-null and avoid assigning null by unlocking and clearing it instead. I’ll revert the nullable annotation, ensure `toEncode` is unlocked and set to a safe state in `clear()`, and guard its use in `encode()` with a local variable and a null check to satisfy NullAway. Then I’ll rebuild to confirm the total error count decreases by one.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/glide && sed -i 's/    @Nullable private ResourceEncoder<Z> encoder;
    @Nullable private LockedResource<Z> toEncode;/    @Nullable private ResourceEncoder<Z> encoder;
    private LockedResource<Z> toEncode;/' library/src/main/java/com/bumptech/glide/load/engine/DecodeJob.java && sed -i '728,740c\    void encode(DiskCacheProvider diskCacheProvider, Options options) {
      ResourceEncoder<Z> localEncoder = encoder;
      LockedResource<Z> localToEncode = toEncode;
      if (localEncoder == null || localToEncode == null) {
        return;
      }
      GlideTrace.beginSection("DecodeJob.encode");
      try {
        diskCacheProvider
            .getDiskCache()
            .put(key, new DataCacheWriter<>(localEncoder, localToEncode, options));
      } finally {
        localToEncode.unlock();
        GlideTrace.endSection();
      }
    }

    boolean hasResourceToEncode() {
      return toEncode != null;
    }

    void clear() {
      key = null;
      encoder = null;
      if (toEncode != null) {
        toEncode.unlock();
      }
      toEncode = null;
    }' library/src/main/java/com/bumptech/glide/load/engine/DecodeJob.java && ./gradlew library:compileDebugJavaWithJavac -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1```

